<b>&lt;b&gt;The order of the Four [Torah] Portions; it contains</b><br> <i data-commentator="Be'er HaGolah" data-label="א" data-order="1"></i> <i data-commentator="Sha'arei Teshuvah" data-order="1"></i> <i data-commentator="Mishnah Berurah" data-label="א"></i> seven clauses.&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br&gt;When Rosh Chodesh Adar that adjoins Nissan falls on Shabbos, we <i data-commentator="Mishnah Berurah" data-label="ב"></i> read the <i data-commentator="Turei Zahav" data-order="1"></i> Torah portion <i data-commentator="Ba'er Hetev" data-order="1"></i> of &lt;i&gt;Shekalim&lt;/i&gt;—that <i data-commentator="Be'er HaGolah" data-label="ב" data-order="2"></i> is, &lt;i&gt;Parashas Ki Tisa&lt;/i&gt; through the verse about making the copper <i data-commentator="Be'er HaGolah" data-label="ג" data-order="3"></i> <i data-commentator="Mishnah Berurah" data-label="ג"></i> laver—and the haftarah is &lt;i&gt;Vayichres Yehoyada&lt;/i&gt;. We take out three Torah scrolls: from the first we read that week’s regular <i data-commentator="Mishnah Berurah" data-label="ד"></i> portion; from <i data-commentator="Mishnah Berurah" data-label="ה"></i> the second, that of Rosh Chodesh; and from the third the maftir reads from &lt;i&gt;Parashas Shekalim&lt;/i&gt;:
